T:What it is
T:Earth is the
T:third planet
T:from the Sun
T:and the only
T:astronomical
T:object known
T:to harbor
T:life. This
T:is made
T:possible by
T:Earth being
T:an ocean
T:world, the
T:only one in
T:the Solar
T:System
T:sustaining
T:liquid
T:surface
T:water.
T:Almost all
T:of Earth's
T:water is
T:contained in
T:its ocean,
T:which covers
T:70.8% of
T:Earth's
T:crust. The
T:remaining
T:29.2% of
T:Earth's
T:crust is
T:land, which
T:is
T:predominantl
T:located
T:within
T:Earth's land
T:hemisphere
T:in the form
T:of
T:continental
T:landmasses.
T:Most of
T:Earth's land
T:is at least
T:somewhat
T:humid and
T:covered by
T:vegetation,
T:while large
T:ice sheets
T:at Earth's
T:polar
T:deserts
T:retain more
T:water than
T:Earth's
T:groundwater,
T:lakes,
T:rivers, and
T:atmospheric
T:water
T:combined.
T:Earth's
T:crust
T:consists of
T:slowly
T:moving
T:tectonic
T:plates,
T:which
T:interact to
T:produce
T:mountain
T:ranges,
T:volcanoes,
T:and
T:earthquakes.
T:Earth has a
T:liquid outer
T:core that
T:generates a
T:magnetospher
T:capable of
T:deflecting
T:most of the
T:destructive
T:solar winds
T:and cosmic
T:radiation.
